+//
+// Animation
+//
+// rEFInd draws straight into a linear framebuffer, so anything that moves has to
+// be composed and blitted by hand. Three things make that affordable at
+// 3840x2160. A tile is 617x617 -- a fortieth of the screen -- so only the parts
+// that change are touched. The frosted background behind a tile depends on where
+// the tile is, not on what frame it is, so it is computed once per position
+// rather than once per frame. And egCopyScreenArea() can read the framebuffer
+// back, so a cross-fade never has to recompose what is already on the screen.
+//
+// Nothing here is ever allowed to get in the way: every loop stops the moment a
+// keystroke is waiting, so holding an arrow key moves exactly as fast as it did
+// before any of this existed.
+
